-- Editor's decision: the site puts the Ethiopic first and does not repeat Platt's opinion that the Ethiopic Didascalia is a loose version of a Greek work. Reword the Didascalia dataset notes and the book's canon note.
UPDATE datasets SET notes = 'Source: Internet Archive scan of the University of California copy, ethiopicdidascal00platrich (Platt, The Ethiopic Didascalia; or, the Ethiopic version of the Apostolical Constitutions, received in the church of Abyssinia. With an English translation. London: published for the Oriental Translation Fund, R. Bentley, 1834). PROVENANCE, from Platt''s own preface (pp. viii-ix): he translated into English an Ethiopic manuscript titled Didascalia, one of several Ethiopic manuscripts obtained at Jerusalem for the Church Missionary Society and given to the British and Foreign Bible Society, in whose library he found it; he laid the translation and the manuscript before the Oriental Translation Committee. The manuscript is the only one he had, and it is mutilated at the close: it ends in the middle of the ''35th Canon, or Section'' of Ludolf''s list, and searches for a fuller copy failed. Platt''s 22 sections are the first 15 canons of Ludolf''s table plus 7 others. The English ends mid-sentence (''Submit yourselves and be obedient unto Rulers, and to . . . . . .''), followed by Platt''s note ''Caetera desunt'' (the rest is wanting). Platt also notes that a leaf is lost from the manuscript at one place (section XVI), where his English breaks with a row of asterisks. NOTE ON PLATT''S VIEW: in his preface (pp. ix-x) Platt compares the Ethiopic with the Greek Apostolic Constitutions and calls the Ethiopic a loose version of it. That is his 1834 opinion, kept here for the record. Kingdom Code puts the Ethiopic first: this edition is shown for what the Ethiopic says, and the site does not repeat his judgment. LOADED: the English text only, 22 sections, about 34,500 words. NOT loaded: the Ge''ez text printed above the English on each page, the preface, the Table of section titles, the notes and the glossary. NUMBERING (ours): the book has no verse numbers. Chapter = Platt''s section number; each verse is one paragraph as indented in the printed book; verse 1 of each section is its printed heading (for section I, after the opening address in chapter 1). METHOD: text taken from the Internet Archive OCR layer (ABBYY), the English lines separated from the Ge''ez by line, paragraphs found from the indentation of each line in the page scan, then corrected for OCR errors by a dictionary check and by comparison with an independent Tesseract OCR run on the page images (33,651 of 33,866 words agree; every difference was resolved against the page image or is a recorded Tesseract error). Pages checked by eye against the scan: 32, 70, 78, 100, 101, 103, 109, 146, 157. NOT yet proofread line by line against the whole scan: treat as a working text before app release. EDITORIAL CHANGES: OCR errors corrected (for example ''hfe'' to ''life'', ''meiTy'' to ''merry'' where the scan reads ''make merry''); a printer''s slip ''hair o thy head'' corrected to ''hair of thy head'' (page 6); spaces before ; : ! ? removed; two capitalised title lines at the start set in ordinary capitals; the ''ae'' ligature kept (Caesar, Zacchaeus as Platt prints them). The bracketed break note in section XVI and the closing bracketed line are Kingdom Code''s wording of Platt''s notes. Platt''s translation is the English of an Ethiopic text: the Ge''ez source label on the site rests on the title page and preface, quoted above.' WHERE code = 'platt_didascalia';
UPDATE books SET canon_note = 'Ethiopic Didascalia: a church order ascribed to the Apostles. Platt''s English is from one incomplete Ethiopic manuscript (22 sections).' WHERE id = 87;
